{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChLkjiTBEmd1fCVR6CWYq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`diff(S*S, t)` now returns `S(t)*Derivative(S(t), t) + Derivative(S(t), t)*S(t)`, which is the expected result. I checked that and `diff(S**3, t)` by hand. I didn't run the pytest suite.\n\nThe fix is in `Pow._eval_derivative` in `sympy/core/power.py`. When the base is non-commutative and the exponent is a positive integer, it now differentiates by the product rule. It sums `base**i * dbase * base**(n-i-1)` over each position `i` in the product, which keeps the factor order. Other cases use the old formula.\n\nNon-commutative bases with a negative or symbolic exponent still use the old formula. They probably give wrong results too, but I left them alone to keep the fix small."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":944,"cache_read_input_tokens":18681,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":944},"output_tokens":258,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ae2f371-2b81-48a2-bcc3-d2119d865fc8","uuid":"acb6654c-3349-4b9a-8421-dc4783482e6b","timestamp":"2026-11-23T23:49:13.546Z","request_id":"req_011ChLkjiKEm6VV2kPopGPFh"}
